  "historical_output_text": " A common weather pattern is a cold front. Imagine a big, cool wall of air moving from the north, pushing into warmer, more humid air. This cool air is heavier and denser than the warm air, so it slides under the warm air and lifts it up. As the warm air rises, it cools and the water vapor in it condenses into clouds. If conditions are right, these clouds can grow into large storm systems, bringing rain, thunderstorms, and sometimes even tornadoes. \n\nCold fronts often bring a sudden drop in temperature, stronger winds, and a change in humidity. The warm, moist air that the cold front pushes away can lead to thunderstorm development later, as the air rises and cools. Cold fronts are a major factor in changing weather patterns and are responsible for many of the storms and precipitation events we see",
